content.text:
Found the real root cause, and it's bigger than a testing quirk — I verified the resolver's actual source (`overrides-plugin.js`), not just the plan's summary of it.

**The mechanism, confirmed directly in code:**

```js
isFromExtends(request, filepath) {
  return (
    this.extends.includes(pkgName) &&
    // request is not issued from overrides
    !filepath.includes(issuerPath)
  );
}
```

The resolver only redirects a `@salesforce/retail-react-app/...` import to our override when the **issuing file is a base-template file** (not inside `overrides/`). The doc comment says it plainly: *"This will return false if the issuer is a file in the overrides dir."*

`overrides/app/pages/product-detail/index.tsx` **is** an overrides file. Its import at line 59 —
```ts
import RecommendedProducts from '@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/recommended-products';
```
— is issued from inside `overrides/`, so `isFromExtends` returns `false` there. That import goes straight to the real, untouched base component in `node_modules`. It was never going to be redirected to our new file, on PDP, ever — regardless of Island, regardless of scroll, regardless of anything we've been chasing. Cart's import is genuinely different: it's issued from `node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/pages/cart/index.jsx`, a real base file, so it correctly redirects — which is exactly what your console confirmed.

**Why this matters beyond today's verification:** as the plan currently stands, this isn't fixed anywhere. A6 only changes `recommended-products/index.tsx` itself; nothing in the plan touches `product-detail/index.tsx`'s *own* import of `RecommendedProducts`. That means even after A6 ships the branch logic, **the PDP rail would never render** — SFCC-83's PDP requirement would silently fail to ship while Cart worked fine. This is a real gap in the approved plan, not a test-environment quirk.

The fix is already documented doctrine, not something new: `overrideStyling.md`'s "Importing an already-overridden component from a new component" rule says exactly this — a component importing another already-overridden component must use a relative path, not the base package path. `product-detail/index.tsx`'s import of `RecommendedProducts` needs that same treatment: change it from package path to relative, pointing at our new file.

That's a new touch to `product-detail/index.tsx` beyond A4's planned "two lines in the Einstein effect" — same file, same Tier 3 scope you already confirmed, just one more specific line. Given how central this is, I want to fold it into the plan properly before continuing rather than patch it silently. Do you want this rolled into A6 (which is already the step that makes the branch functionally matter), or should it be its own step?

